Z||000005||Dan||09-11-1999||12:17 AM||||so how does a Fiero hold up to unannounced visitors at the back door?||||reg||1
Z||000006||BOILERMAKER||09-13-1999||08:47 AM||||Well, he hit me pretty square and hard.  I have some frame damage only on the framehorns, so it isn't affecting the alignment or anything like that.  The total came out to $1700 and I am still driving it.  I am waiting on the insurance company (his) to get me the check so I can get it in.  If he would have hit me at an angle, I could've kissed this car goodbye.  <P>The car is good on accidents as far as I've seen.  It crumples up around you and not on you.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dan||09-27-1999||06:13 PM|||| Was working on the Fiero fleet and a guy comes up and says he has a wrecked 86gt front right damage. Says he's gonna pull the engine cradle and I can have the rest. Gee, free advertising for free parts. The 86 was a pretty car, silver.Oh well, lots of nice parts. ||||reg||10
Z||000008||WGraffiKs||09-27-1999||09:20 PM||||Gee, I guess I'm WAAAAAaaay behind, I only have two 86.5 GT's!!!  One red, one white.  Too bad they didn't offer the Blue until 87!<BR>The sad thing, I primarily use the Yamaha as my "daily cruiser".  Sure beats sitting in "parking-lot" traffic for 38 miles EACH WAY, though! (I'll admit, it may also be a bit faster than my GT 0-60, too)  But for those nights when style and head-turning are in order......FIERO time!!!!||||reg||4
